		<!-- The picture closes the left column on the line the map above declares, with HUMANITY
		     and SPECIALTIES (SPEC T134, V69). It has followed that line since the 50th round.

		     The FRAME is the rectangle below, and it carries the same four numbers as the image
		     on purpose (SPEC V309, B71): it is a loose <rectangle>, so the box collector of I73
		     never sees it, and three rounds of gap tightening moved the image while it stayed at
		     top=471 - thirty pixels of grey hanging under the photo, green in the gate the whole
		     time. V309 is what makes that impossible to repeat; do not move one without the other.

		     It keeps color="DimGray" and is the only carrier of that key in the four palettes
		     (SPEC V53, T653). The radius matches the section boxes so the corner reads as one
		     frame family. -->
		<rectangle name="avatarFrame" left="0" top="431" width="340" height="457" color="DimGray" xradius="14" yradius="14" cornerType="innerRound"/>
		<image left="0" top="431" width="340" height="457" field="avatar" editable="true" style="autoFit"/>

		<!-- The ornament CARRIER (SPEC I84d, I92, V309, V287, V320). Transparent in both senses
		      and holding no content of its own: it exists so the era's drawing has somewhere to hang
		      ABOVE the photo, which is what declaring it AFTER the image buys.

		      It is one of the TWO names sectionBox answers for (SPEC I92a, V320a). By CONSTRUCTION it
		      is indistinguishable from the nineteen tabOn<X> carriers - transparent both ways, its own
		      radius, hitTest false - so a fill-based arm would bevel the tab pills along with it, and
		      their width is priced for the concave arc (SPEC V228, V316d). That is why the arm is a
		      declared roster and not a rule about shape.

		      It is also the only one of the avatar's three controls that DRAWS. The frame above sits
		      UNDER the image, so a path hung on it lands behind the photo - which is exactly B75, the
		      round where this carrier was authored for a mechanism that refused it and drew nothing at
		      all for five rounds.

		      It carries the picture's own four numbers, which V309 measures across all THREE controls.
		      THREE palettes declare ornament - Victorian filigree, Dark Ages filete, Modern Nights
		      corrente - so the Classical Age shows no drawing here and that is correct (SPEC V277,
		      V320f). -->
		<rectangle name="ornAvatar" left="0" top="431" width="340" height="457" color="#00000000" strokeColor="#00000000" xradius="14" yradius="14" cornerType="innerRound" hitTest="false"/>

		<!--
			The three ability columns stand loose (SPEC T407, V168, V169). Until the 49th round
			they sat inside one ABILITIES box, whose frame and title spent 30px of width on a
			heading that named what the three titles below it already said. The user asked for
			the heading gone so the names could breathe, so the box the columns were nested in
			is now the row they form: TALENTS starts where VIRTUES starts and KNOWLEDGES closes
			where EXPERIENCE closes. V168 measures it against those two boxes, not against a
			literal, so moving either one without the other turns the gate red.

			Each column still fills black under a centred title of its own width, so V27 and V48
			read them exactly as before. What changed is V40: it now weighs them against every
			box on the tab instead of against the two siblings they shared a frame with - which
			is the check the old nesting was hiding (SPEC B18 is the same lesson from the fill
			side, and the frame that carried that note went out with this comment).

			The gap between columns is 20, the gap I73 gives every pair of boxes, and each row inside
			a column is 290 wide at left=20 - the width the column gained arrives as NAME, never as air
			between the last dot and the border (SPEC V170, I73).
